| Recipe | New citrus architecture | Citrus-group dose | Reason |
|---|---|---:|---|
| Crystalline Iris Violet Mineral Vetiver Skin V7 | CIT-C1 Bergamot Floral Peel Core 203 µL + CIT-A1 Leaf–Blossom Handoff Adapter 36 µL | 239 µL | Bergamot-floral peel supports crystalline iris; the small leaf/blossom adapter prevents a detached top. |
| Rainmetal Violet Mineral Muguet Cedar Musk | CIT-C5 Green Lime–Citron Core 211 µL + CIT-A1 Leaf–Blossom Handoff Adapter 70 µL | 281 µL | Green lime/citron creates rain-cold brightness; the leaf/blossom adapter carries it into muguet. |
| Pear Violet Suede Polished Vetiver Skin | CIT-C3 Grapefruit Bitter–Rhubarb Core 305 µL | 305 µL | A single grapefruit-rhubarb branch reinforces pear/violet motion without generic lemon-orange clutter. |
| White Tea Jasmine Pale Suede Vetiver Skin | CIT-C1 Bergamot Floral Peel Core 288 µL + CIT-A1 Leaf–Blossom Handoff Adapter 72 µL | 360 µL | Bergamot remains dominant for the tea-jasmine direction; the adapter supplies a controlled leaf/floral seam. |
| Aldehydic Peony Iris Linen Sandal Vetiver Musk | CIT-C1 Bergamot Floral Peel Core 295 µL | 295 µL | A narrow bergamot core leaves the separate aldehydic group free to create linen shimmer without citrus duplication. |
| Apricot White Floral Violet Suede Musk | CIT-C4 Warm Orange Peel Core 264 µL | 264 µL | Warm orange peel supports apricot and white floral; no grapefruit or petitgrain is added by default. |
| Night Tuberose Orange Flower Incense Leather V7 | CIT-C4 Warm Orange Peel Core 175 µL + CIT-A1 Leaf–Blossom Handoff Adapter 75 µL | 250 µL | Warm orange plus a blossom adapter directly supports tuberose/orange flower rather than an unrelated grapefruit/citron mix. |
| Dark Root Orris Guaiac Incense Suede Skin | CIT-C3 Grapefruit Bitter–Rhubarb Core 174 µL + CIT-A3 Green Resin–Spice Handoff Adapter 74 µL | 248 µL | Bitter grapefruit-rhubarb plus resin/spice creates a dark transition into guaiac, incense and orris. |
| Cedrat Pink Pepper Aromatic Vetiver Linen | CIT-C2 Dry Citron–Lemon Peel Core 496 µL + CIT-A3 Green Resin–Spice Handoff Adapter 124 µL | 620 µL | Dry citron-lemon is the target identity; the resin/spice adapter connects into pink pepper, vetiver and aromatic woods. |
| Magnolia Orange Blossom Sandal Incense Skin | CIT-C1 Bergamot Floral Peel Core 119 µL + CIT-A1 Leaf–Blossom Handoff Adapter 178 µL | 297 µL | The leaf/blossom adapter is dominant because magnolia/orange flower is the target; bergamot is only a luminous peel frame. |

## Mixing and storage

1. Prepare any child stocks first, especially Citral Natural 10% in DEP.
2. For each module, add persistent specialty/product-basis materials first, then oxygenated bridges, then major peel oils, then aldehydes and ultra-traces.
3. Mix gently and store in a full, tightly closed amber bottle protected from light and heat.
4. Record parent ExactStockRefs and a module preparation receipt. Do not mark a module as physically present until this exists.
5. Re-smell after 24–72 h before integrating into a perfume.

## Controlled validation

- Compare each species core against the old all-citrus bundle at equal perceived opening intensity.
- Test core alone, core + adapter at 3:1, 1:1 and 1:3, and adapter alone.
- Evaluate 0, 5, 30 and 120 minutes; repeat on another day.
- Record peel identity, bitterness, juiciness, floral/leaf transition, cleaner character, species recognition, persistence and explicit preference.
- For grapefruit, separately omit Methyl Pamplemousse and Paradisamide.
- For bergamot and warm orange, separately omit the trace aldehyde/fruit detail rows.
- Run an accelerated light/air comparison only as a degradation screen, not as normal storage.

## Evidence ledger

| Source | Supports | Does not support | URL |
|---|---|---|---|
| González-Mas et al., 2019 | Review of volatile compounds across ten Citrus species; species differ substantially and hundreds of volatiles are species-specific. | Does not provide a fine-fragrance formula or universal dose. | https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2019.00012 |
| Sawamura et al., 2006 | Bergamot GC-sniffing/AEDA and aroma model; bergamot-like cues include limonene oxide, decanal, linalyl acetate and geraniol. | Food/aroma-model context is not direct validation of this module in perfume. | https://doi.org/10.1002/ffj.1604 |
| Xiao et al., 2016 | Sweet-orange GC-MS/GC-O correlates characteristic aroma with limonene and trace aldehydes/terpenes rather than limonene alone. | Does not establish perfume use levels. | https://doi.org/10.1002/ffj.3268 |
| Xiao et al., 2017 | Mandarin recombination and omission identified nonanal, hexanal, linalool and limonen as key, with beta-ionone, decanal, gamma-terpinene and methyl butyrate also important. | Juice evidence does not prove that adding those molecules creates mandarin perfume; current branch is therefore not called mandarin. | https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jafc.7b02703 |
| Buettner & Schieberle, 2001 | Grapefruit reconstitution showed critical roles for sulfurous grapefruit odorants plus fruity esters and other traces. | Current inventory lacks those exact sulfur materials; Methyl Pamplemousse/Paradisamide are not declared equivalent. | https://doi.org/10.1021/jf001235x |
| Schulze et al., 2024 | Lime AEDA/recombination identified alpha-pinene, limonene, gamma-terpinene and 7-methoxycoumarin as important to Citrus latifolia aroma. | The 36-component food-aroma recombination is not copied into a perfume module. | https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jafc.4c02059 |
| Boussaada & Chemli, 2006 | Neroli, petitgrain and peel oils from the same plant organ differ strongly; leaf and flower oils should be treated as different functions. | Compositional percentages vary by origin/lot and do not set perfume dose. | https://doi.org/10.1080/0972060X.2006.10643484 |
| Liu et al., 2016 | Air/UV transformation of citrus aldehydes and citral reduced lemon/herbal aroma; storage and oxidation control matter. | Pummelo oil is not identical to every citrus oil. | https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jafc.6b01369 |
| Givaudan Methyl Pamplemousse | Official profile: fresh bitter grapefruit peel; modern cologne and vetiver booster; 0.1–10% supplier use range. | Supplier description is not proof of beauty or exact dosage in Kenny's perfumes. | https://www.givaudan.com/fragrance-beauty/fragrance-ingredients-business/fragrance-molecules/methyl-pamplemousse |
| Givaudan Paradisamide | Official profile: long-lasting guava, grapefruit, rhubarb and cassis; supplier documents synergy with Methyl Pamplemousse. | A supplier synergy is precedent, not physical validation of this module. | https://www.givaudan.com/fragrance-beauty/fragrance-ingredients-business/fragrance-molecules/paradisamidetm |

## Authority boundary

- This is a computational architecture and bench plan.
- Physical liking, strict empirical OAV, measured headspace, safety, stability and release remain **NOT TESTED**.
- Food-aroma recombination research is used to identify causal odor functions, not copied as a fine-fragrance formula.
- Supplier descriptions and demo synergies are precedent, not proof of hedonic superiority.
